WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.610
Film Courage: How would you handle if you
were working on a project with great people

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:10.800
(magical, great chemistry) but they're a little
bit behind on paying you?

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.060
How would you approach that?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.360
Brad Rushing, Cinematographer: What do you
mean by a little bit behind?

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.240
Film Courage: A couple months?

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.340
Brad: That would probably be a long project.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.999
I'd hand it off to my agent.

00:00:24.999 --> 00:00:32.290
I'd tell my agent to deal with it and they
have a legal team and I would keep doing I

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:37.640
mean I certainly wouldn't confront any of
my creative partners or probably even tell

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.480
them about it.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.730
I mean I might if I felt they would have influence
with the production company but I wouldn't

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:45.730
threaten them.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:49.780
I wouldn't be one of those guys like I'm going
to walk off again.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.230
I'm committed to the work first and even if
even if those good-for-nothings never paid

00:00:54.230 --> 00:01:01.870
me I'd finish the job and never work with
them again but to me somebody else's bad behavior

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:07.240
is not a license for me to also have bad behavior.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:13.920
Again if somebody's being unsafe if somebody's
being jeopardized or harassed or harmed that

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.200
would be different I would certainly become
defensive and confrontational in that responsibility

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:29.009
as to whatever degree was necessary in the
moment but by and large I believe in diplomacy

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:35.039
and professionalism and if it's just that
my pay is late I certainly wouldn't be happy

00:01:35.039 --> 00:01:40.139
about it but I'm not going to harm the work
and I'm not going to harm my relationship

00:01:40.139 --> 00:01:44.159
with the parties who are not directly involved
in that predicament.

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:50.039
Film Courage: How about earlier in your career
when you did not have an agent?

00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:57.340
Brad: Well it would still be a similar strategy
but I would not jeopardize my position on

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:05.119
the project I would I would not go into defcon
mode until after the project wrapped and even

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:12.330
then I wouldn't be impolitic about it I would
I would be professional I would I would give

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:18.810
them notice and then I would either get a
lawyer or I would file a complaint with the

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:25.430
labor board I would do the right things if
I was in the union I would or I mean I am

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.100
in the union now but if I was at the time
I would report it to the union and that's

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.760
the other thing too is if I was on that job
in addition to seeking my agent on them I

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.481
would stick the union on them but if I was
all on my own I would like I said I would

00:02:39.481 --> 00:02:46.010
just wait until I would not be happy but I
would put up with it until we were done and

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:52.860
then I would go through the the legal avenues
available to me and if I ended up getting

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.820
boned in the end and even if I didn't even
if they ended up paying if I had a bad experience

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:03.480
and felt they were being predatory I would
just never work with them again to answer

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:09.930
that it's like look in in withholding my pay
they're already harming me right but if I

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:19.610
relieve in my own well then that's adding
harm if I provoke them in such a way that

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:26.200
they spread bad stories about me even if they're
in the wrong I don't know who they know if

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:33.980
the producer or the director or my the actors
if I create a situation where they think I

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:39.189
have some bad behavior that's gonna harm me
I don't there's no value in that to me that's

00:03:39.189 --> 00:03:44.880
just that's just adding adding my own harm
to it and I just don't think it's gonna improve

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.709
it and that's that's just based on my value
because again I'm their first and foremost

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:55.010
for the work and the relationships how much
time do you spend each day improving your

00:03:55.010 --> 00:04:00.230
craft as a cinematographer well see that's
one of those formula questions there's there's

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:06.220
just no formulas there is no bullet point
on my calendar every day

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:19.359
I nebulously engage I read the news I read
IMDB PRO news I engage on social media I belong

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:28.810
to networking groups I belong to groups specifically
about film lenses I belong to cinematographer

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:34.259
groups I belong to professional groups like
the Canadian society cinematographers the

00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:39.490
visual effects society and I what are people
talking about what are the articles they're

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:46.280
posting I might come across an article and
post it so it's really I mean I do my due

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:52.760
diligence and I do my check-ins but if it's
a day where there's really nothing on those

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:57.780
boards or on the news then then I probably
don't do too much if it's a day that there's

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:03.349
significant amounts of stuff if I have the
time then then I'll be watching some videos

00:05:03.349 --> 00:05:11.290
and reading some articles maybe bookmarking
if I don't have the time I do I do I'll do

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:18.710
webinars and workshops and in in a normal
area era where we're not in a in a pandemic

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:25.889
I'll go to trade shows and product demos which
in as much as they're important to learning

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:31.430
about your craft and the new technologies
they're also networking opportunities and

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.840
it's fun to me it's not it's not it's not
work I mean again these are these are toys

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.840
it's like what's the new toy what's the cool
thing it's instead of a 4k camera you have

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:48.970
an 8k camera and why should I care and now
you've got these affordable anamorphic lenses

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:54.500
where did those come from and how are they
different from the tens of thousands of dollars

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:01.540
lenses and now you've got these rgb leds that
could do any color under the sun and they

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:08.650
can do a cop light and they can do a strobe
and they can do fire flicker and I just I

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:17.060
love that because cool that's a new toy and
I either rent it or go out and buy it so again

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:24.669
there's no I can't tell you how many hours
a day but it's certainly one of my focuses

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:32.009
it's one of the things that just like when
somebody reads the news they check the the

00:06:32.009 --> 00:06:37.890
the national section and the international
section and the the comics and the one ads

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:43.090
for instance it's like that's one of those
things that every day I'm like okay what's

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:48.270
what do I need to check with how much time
do you spend trying to book your next job

00:06:48.270 --> 00:07:00.069
i could say 24 7 considering I have a website
I have I have posts and and social media identities

00:07:00.069 --> 00:07:06.650
on linkedin and to me that's like little fish
poles with bait that are stuck out into the

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:10.229
into the water 24 7.

00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:14.860
I network I plant those seeds I have people
out there that I hope are recommending me

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:21.720
and saying good things maybe you would call
that passive but to me that's all sometimes

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:27.310
those would get me more work than me picking
up a phone or or asking or going to lunch

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:32.009
it's those it's those those ripples you put
out into the world just like you obviously

00:07:32.009 --> 00:07:37.150
did your research on my website I didn't have
anything to do with that I mean I created

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:43.500
it but I you and I didn't talk about it in
terms of in terms of me personally it really

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:49.930
depends on the days when when when California
Christmas was blowing up I was looking at

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:55.750
IMDB films in pre-production and just reaching
out to people because I thought now's the

00:07:55.750 --> 00:08:01.819
moment I can reach out and say hey I've got
a number one film but you have to balance

00:08:01.819 --> 00:08:08.990
that with life and I could easily be a workaholic
as I've already kind of let that I would work

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:22.030
through meals and through the night and I
network and hustle far more than I shoot there's

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:33.250
this great quote by Orson Welles and it's
something to the effect that he he he probably

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:41.550
made a mistake becoming a filmmaker because
it's two percent making movies and 98 begging

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:48.899
for the money to make them I'm paraphrasing
but that's the essence of part of that quote

00:08:48.899 --> 00:08:53.930
and it really is I mean so much of our work
is getting work what I like to tell people

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:59.529
that I am professionally unemployed because
what most people do only a few times in their

00:08:59.529 --> 00:09:06.720
life in between jobs I do constantly even
when I'm on a job I'm looking for work so

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:15.149
so really if you looked at how I spend the
hours in in my day I'm professionally unemployed

00:09:15.149 --> 00:09:18.670
when you first started out was it the same
percentage of time that you were looking for

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:28.910
work absolutely not and that's naivete I really
felt build it and they will come I really

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:36.450
felt that I mean I had never been a freelancer
before I didn't understand the dynamic of

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:41.690
working as a freelancer I didn't understand
the importance of networking I had only ever

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:46.670
worked for a company where they meet you once
and they hire you and then you just keep showing

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:55.440
up and doing the time card until you quit
or get fired so it really was a huge learning

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:02.370
curve for me and there were a lot of illusions
just like when I moved to Los Angeles I thought

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:07.350
oh it's a huge city somebody's going to hire
me somebody's going to give me a job and I

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:15.120
was so full of malarkey and I guess mercifully
so because because if I really realized how

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.070
hard it was going to be would I have done
it probably but it I wouldn't have been as

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:24.360
hopeful and optimistic it would have been
more oppressive to live with the reality of

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:28.030
what lay ahead of me instead of being blissfully
ignorant.

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:34.500
Film Courage: Do you think working at these
temp jobs prepared you because it's the same

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:39.269
thing like when you would work at these temp
jobs and one of these things would end and

00:10:39.269 --> 00:10:43.140
okay accounting doesn't need you anymore I
don't know what you were doing but and then

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:47.800
you had to go and let the temp company know
I'm available this week?

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:55.120
Brad: Yeah I mean in little ways I think because
because like you could wait for them to call

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:00.600
you if they had a job for you but you could
also wake up at 6 00 a.m and call and say

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:05.850
I'm available and you were more likely to
get something so there was that incentive

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:13.089
be proactive and I'm not a morning person
I mean left to my own devices I wake up at

00:11:13.089 --> 00:11:19.180
10 so for me to wake up at 6 00 a.m that was
a super human feat it's not so hard if it's

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:24.760
a call time and I'm getting paid but just
to call wake up and make a call because maybe

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:31.360
I'll get paid to go to a job that I really
don't want to do oh my goodness yes well we

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:36.019
also don't want to go to the clinic and get
shots but we have to so it's just I'm at that

00:11:36.019 --> 00:11:41.560
age now you have to get a colonoscopy every
five years it's I'd rather go to Disneyland

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:47.339
but there are things in life you have to do
and when I was out here calling the temp agency

00:11:47.339 --> 00:11:52.540
at 6am was one of them and it was again it
was a good habit it was discipline it's like

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:57.870
do that thing you don't want to do because
you have to no your boss isn't telling you

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:03.839
to and your mom and dad and girlfriend aren't
telling you to and if you don't nobody's going

00:12:03.839 --> 00:12:05.723
to be mad at you you just aren't going to
work.

00:12:05.723 --> 00:12:07.370
